Server testing
==============

   USING:
   =====
   ./testserver.py SERVER:PATH <options> <tests>
   ./testserver.py SERVER:PATH <--rundeps | --force> <--maketree> -v all
   --maketree Needs to be run the first time testserver is used, creates
              directory/file structure most tests expect
   --force    Run exactly the tests listed on command-line, ignoring 
              dependencies. NOTE that the default is to try to run exactly 
	      the tests listed, but if a test's dependencies are not run the 
	      test will be skipped
   --rundeps  In addition to tests listed on command-line, run tests that
              are listed as dependencies.
   -v         Show tests as they are being run
   --noinit   Skip initial cleanup/testing of directory tree.  This is useful
              for quickly running tests that do not need it, such as most
              of the exchangeid tests.
  Tests are indicated by either a CODE (unique to a test) or a FLAG (which
     is a way of grouping similar tests).  The list of flags can be seen
     using the --showflags option.  Each code/flag can be preceeded by "no"
     to prevent running of that test.  code/flags are read from left to
     right, and effects are cumulative.  Thus:
         ./testserver SERVER:PATH --force -v all noopen OPEN1
     will run all tests, except for the open tests, but will run the test OPEN1

  All tests are located in nfs4/server41tests/st_*.py
  Generic test configuration is done in nfs4/server41tests/environment.py
      init/finish - routines run at start/end of program
      startUp/shutDown - routines run at start/end of each test
      In particular, tests use two principals, they are hardcoded currently 
          nfs4/server41tests/environment.py:Environment.__init__()

  MISC:
  =====
  Current tests are fairly minimal, as spec has been changing fairly rapidly.
  Good for correctness testing, handling of error/unlikely paths, any test
     where it is the order of RPCs that matter
  Not good for performance testing, tests where timing of RPC's matter.
  Checking state of returned RPC's can be tedious, so tests work best
     if need only check return stat or a few flags.
   It is possible to send individual commands by hand using 
       nfs4/nfs4client.py:NFS4Client(), but requires a bit of familiarity with code.


RPC over RDMA
=============

  The client side of pynfs (the library and the tests that use it) can talk
  to a server over RPC-over-RDMA version 1 (RFC 8166) instead of TCP:

      ./testserver.py --rdma SERVER[:PORT]/PATH <options> <tests>
      ./read_example.py --rdma SERVER /export/file

  The port is 20049, the usual one for NFS over RDMA, unless the URL gives
  another.  In your own code: NFS4Client(host, port, minorversion, rdma=True).
  NFSv4.1 and 4.2 only; nfs4.0, nfs3client and the proxy and data server do
  not use it.

  REQUIREMENTS
  ============
  librdmacm and libibverbs from rdma-core (Debian: librdmacm1 libibverbs1
  ibverbs-providers; Fedora: librdmacm libibverbs).  Nothing else: the
  transport reaches them with ctypes (rpc/rdma_verbs.py), loaded the first
  time an RDMA connection is made, so nothing changes for TCP.  The struct
  offsets in that file come from the rdma-core headers and are part of the
  stable libibverbs/librdmacm ABI; they have been tried against rdma-core 28.

  SETTING UP A SERVER
  ===================
  Linux nfsd (it must also allow connections from high ports: "insecure"):
      modprobe rpcrdma
      echo "rdma 20049" > /proc/fs/nfsd/portlist
  With no RDMA hardware, soft-RoCE is enough, and is what this was developed
  against (a Linux nfsd 5.15):
      rdma link add rxe0 type rxe netdev eth0
  The client needs memory it can pin: see RLIMIT_MEMLOCK ("ulimit -l")
  below.

  HOW IT WORKS (rpc/rdma.py: RdmaPipe, a drop-in replacement for RpcPipe)
  ==============
  * Connection setup sends the RFC 8797 private data and takes the server's
    answer to size what can go inline: 4096 bytes with nfsd, 1024 if the
    server sends none.
  * A call that fits is one RDMA_MSG.  A bigger one that is mostly the data
    of a single WRITE goes as an RDMA_MSG without that data, plus a Read chunk
    at the position where the data belongs (RFC 8166, RFC 8267).  The data,
    with its XDR pad, is registered where it already is in the packed call,
    not copied, and the server reads just that into its own buffers.
    NFS4Client passes the data as the write_data hint, only for AUTH_NONE or
    AUTH_SYS and only for a COMPOUND with one WRITE.  If the transport is not
    sure where the data is, or it does not all fit, or memory can not be
    registered, the call goes the way any other big call does:
    as RDMA_NOMSG with the whole call in a Position Zero Read chunk, copied
    to a registered buffer, which the server reads.
  * Every call carries a Reply chunk, for a reply that does not fit inline.
    Its size comes from the call: NFS4Client bounds the reply from the ops
    (nfs4client.reply_bound) and the session's maximum response size caps
    it.  Whatever cannot be bounded gets the session maximum, 64 KB..4 MB.
  * A READ of more than 2 KB, alone in its COMPOUND and over AUTH_NONE or
    AUTH_SYS, also carries a Write chunk: the server writes the data
    straight into our memory (RFC 8267) and the reply has the length but
    not the bytes.  The transport hands the data up with the record and
    nfs4lib.FancyNFS4Unpacker puts it back into the READ result.
  * nfsd wants the Reply chunk to hold the whole reply, READ data included,
    even with a Write chunk, and drops the connection if it does not.  So
    the READ count is part of the size, and when the rest of the reply is
    known to fit inline there is no Reply chunk at all.
  * The backchannel uses the same connection: calls from the server arrive
    inline and are answered inline.  Credits for the two directions are kept
    apart (RFC 8167): the forward window is the server's grant capped to the
    receive buffers not set aside for callbacks.
  * When the peer disconnects or the QP breaks, every call waiting for a
    reply fails at once with RdmaError (not after pynfs's 300 s timeout),
    later calls fail immediately, and everything is freed.  There is no
    reconnect.  A call that only times out leaves the connection alone.
  * One thread waits on the completion channel and the connection-manager
    events and takes the replies; nothing polls.

  MEMORY
  ======
  Chunk buffers are pinned and count against RLIMIT_MEMLOCK.  Buffers are
  page aligned and idle ones are pooled (at most 4 MB).  When one cannot be
  registered a call waits for another to finish, then makes do with a smaller
  Reply chunk; a reply too big for it then fails with RdmaError.  The sizes
  are constants at the top of rpc/rdma.py.

  LIMITATIONS
  ===========
  * Only one WRITE per COMPOUND can be sent in place; a COMPOUND with
    several big ones, or any other big call (SETXATTR, SETATTR with a big
    ACL), is copied into one registered buffer.  (A Linux nfsd stores the
    second of two big WRITEs in one COMPOUND wrongly even over TCP.)
    READ_PLUS, several READs in one COMPOUND and big
    READDIR/READLINK/GETXATTR results come back through the Reply chunk,
    copied.
  * "In place" avoids copying the data into a second registered buffer.
    The packer still builds the call in Python, so this is not zero-copy
    from the application.
  * Callbacks, and the replies to them, must fit inline.
  * No reconnect and no keepalive: a peer that vanishes without
    disconnecting is only noticed when the next send fails.
  * The Reply chunk rule above is what nfsd 5.15 does.  Only that nfsd, over
    soft-RoCE, has been tried; other servers, iWARP, real hardware and
    RPCSEC_GSS have not (GSS gets no Write chunks).
  * Idle pooled buffers stay remotely writable until they are freed.

  TESTS OF THE TRANSPORT
  ======================
  nfs4.1/rdmatests holds unit tests.  From the nfs4.1 directory:

      python3 -m unittest discover -s rdmatests -t . -v

  The ones that need no server always run (test_reply_bound.py,
  test_transport_units.py).  The rest are skipped unless told where a server
  is, by environment variables:

      PYNFS_RDMA_SERVER=host[:port]   a server listening for RDMA
      PYNFS_EXPORT=/path              a writable directory on it
      PYNFS_UID, PYNFS_GID            AUTH_SYS identity (default: ours; it
                                      must be allowed to write there)
      PYNFS_FAKE_ADDR=addr            an address of a local RDMA interface

  They create and remove files in PYNFS_EXPORT, and assume a server that
  honours Write chunks, as a Linux nfsd does.

      test_data.py          data of every size survives, inline, in Read,
                            Reply and Write chunks, and WRITE data sent in
                            place (including odd lengths and results after
                            the READ or WRITE)
      test_chunk_sizes.py   which chunk buffers each kind of call registers
      test_connections.py   extra connections and several sessions; leaks
      test_memory.py        little lockable memory (lowers RLIMIT_MEMLOCK
                            for the process)
      test_failure.py       connection loss and credits, against
                            fakeserver.py, a fake server; needs
                            PYNFS_FAKE_ADDR and the pyverbs python bindings
                            (python3-pyverbs), and is skipped without them

  The pynfs server tests themselves run over RDMA the same way as over TCP
  (testserver.py --rdma); only the groups that need a server restart
  (reboot) have not been tried.


Server
======

  nfs4/nfs4server --help
  nfs4/nfs4server --reset

  Passed all the (non-locking) cthon tests with draft-10 
     (except for large file test), with both auth_sys and krb5
  
  Limitations:
      So far focused on session/pnfs ops, so:
          other ops (in particular locking) haven't been implemented yet
	  ACCESS control is a huge stub
      Unused structures are never cleaned up...so will eventually run out
          of memory
      SLOW
      Multi-user access has never been tested, and is likely buggy

  PNFS-block:
      Has some very basic facilities for handling block layouts
      Currently functions, as long as client behaves

  PNFS-files:
      Support for pnfs-files has been added (--use_files, --is_ds flags).
      See README-filelayout

  Client-testing:
      More difficult, since control over what RPCs are sent is so indirect.
      Write to /config/ files, changes server behavior
      	    PRO - nothing special needed on client side
	    CON - need enough functionality to write to file
      Communicate via side-channel.
      	    PRO - allows full control of server, even if client is 
	              completely non-functional
		  Is easier to control more complex behavior.
	    CON - Have to use python test framework on client
      Possibilities - simulate server reboot, cause error return on given ops,
      		      give client userland-access to the (unencrypted)
		      RPCs it sent.

Proxy
======

	nfs4proxy --port=<local port> --dserver=<destination IP> --dport=<destination port>
		  --errorfile=<file having error description>

	The proxy forwards the calls form the client to the server. It can be used with any server and client. Also, it can be used with pNFS if the server and the dataserver are colocated

     Error injection framework:
	   The user needs to write to a file (errorfile parameter, default error.xml)
	   The format of the xml file is:
	   <errorconf>
	   <error>
	   <name>the name of the error condition</name>
	   <operation>operation this error condition applies to</operation>
	   <operation>can be more than one</operation>
	   <errorcode>error code to be returned</errorcode>
	   <errorcode>more than one can be specified (picks one randomly)</errorcode>
	   <delay>delay this operation by that many seconds</delay>
	   <frequency>
	   probabability for the error to be applied
	   1 = 1/1 (100%)
	   2 = 1/2 (50%)
	   3 = 1/3 (33%)
	   etc.
	   </frequency>
	   <function>function name from errorfunctions.py to be applied if errorcode
	   is not specified e.g. shortread</funtion>
	   </error>
	   <error>
	   another error condition -- apply in sequential order
	   ...
	   </error>

	   example error.xml:
	   <errorconf>
	   <error>
	   <name>sequence1</name>
	   <operation>READ</operation>
	   <function>short_read</function>
	   <delay>0</delay>
	   <frequency>1</frequency>
	   </error>
	   <error>
	   <name>create session</name>
	   <operation>CREATE_SESSION</operation>
	   <operation>SEQUENCE</operation>
	   <errorcode>NFS4ERR_DELAY</errorcode>
	   <errorcode>NFS4ERR_BADSESSION</errorcode>
	   <delay>0</delay>
	   <frequency>2</frequency>
	   </error>
	   </errorconf>